# Materials and Techniques for Implantable Nutrient Sensing Using Flexible Sensors Integrated with Metal-Organic Frameworks

## abstract
The combination of novel materials with flexible electronic technology may yield new concepts of flexible electronic devices that effectively detect various biological chemicals to facilitate understanding of biological processes and conduct health monitoring. This paper demonstrates single- or multichannel implantable flexible sensors that are surface modified with conductive metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) such as copper-MOF and cobalt-MOF with large surface area, high porosity, and tunable catalysis capability. The sensors can monitor important nutriments such as ascorbicacid, glycine, l-tryptophan (l-Trp), and glucose with detection resolutions of 14.97, 0.71, 4.14, and 54.60 × 10-6 m, respectively. In addition, they offer sensing capability even under extreme deformation and complex surrounding environment with continuous monitoring capability for 20 d due to minimized use of biological active chemicals. Experiments using live cells and animals indicate that the MOF-modified sensors are biologically safe to cells, and can detect l-Trp in blood and interstitial fluid. This work represents the first effort in integrating MOFs with flexible sensors to achieve highly specific and sensitive implantable electrochemical detection and may inspire appearance of more flexible electronic devices with enhanced capability in sensing, energy storage, and catalysis using various properties of MOFs.

The combination of novel materials with flexible electronic technology may yield new concepts of flexible electronic devices that effectively detect various biological chemicals to facilitate understanding of biological processes and conduct health monitoring. This paper demonstrates single- or multichannel implantable flexible sensors that are surface modified with conductive metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) such as copper-MOF and cobalt-MOF with large surface area, high porosity, and tunable catalysis capability. The sensors can monitor important nutriments such as ascorbic acid, glycine, l-tryptophan (l-Trp), and glucose with detection resolutions of 14.97, 0.71, 4.14, and $54.60\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~m}$ , respectively. In addition, they offer sensing capability even under extreme deformation and complex surrounding environment with continuous monitoring capability for 20 d due to minimized use of biological active chemicals. Experiments using live cells and animals indicate that the MOF-modified sensors are biologically safe to cells, and can detect l-Trp in blood and interstitial fluid. This work represents the first effort in integrating MOFs with flexible sensors to achieve highly specific and sensitive implantable electrochemical detection and may inspire appearance of more flexible electronic devices with enhanced capability in sensing, energy storage, and catalysis using various properties of MOFs.
The emerging flexible electronics technology offers excellent solutions to construct lightweight and ultrathin implantable devices that can be used to conduct continuous monitoring of analytes in different body fluids and organs to gain profound insights of biophysiological processes[1] and reveal underlying mechanisms of various diseases.[2] The advantages of flexible implantable devices involve reduced tissue damage and conformable tissue contact, leading to high measurement sensitivity and repeatability. In addition, bioinspired flexible tentacle-like structures, which contain bendable and deformable channels scattering from a center controller, can also be constructed, leading to multi­ channel sensing capability for different body or organ locations.
Flexible sensors are notable for their sensing abilities of physical properties such as heart rate,[3] biopotential,[4] temperature,[4] and skin impedance.[5] However, chemical detection using flexible sensors are still largely unexplored other than some notable demonstrations in detecting glucose,[6] lactate,[7,8] and electrolytes.[7,9] Despite excellent adaptability to various surface morphology and environmental conditions, the chemical sensing capability in terms of resolution, specificity, and reliability of flexible sensors may not necessarily outweigh electronic devices based on rigid composition materials.
A promising approach to improve the chemical sensing capability of flexible sensors involves combining these devices with wild ranges of functional materials that offer unique properties to facilitate chemical analysis,[10] resulting in combined advantages both from the functionality of the materials and soft, flexible mechanics of the sensors. A large category of functional materials that have not yet been explored in flexible electronics are metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which are essentially inorganic–organic hybrids that contain repeated metal ions connected with organic ligands. MOFs are notable for their large surface area, highly porous structures, and tunable catalysis capability, all of which are highly demanded for biological sensing. When proper organic ligands are chosen, MOFs can be conductive or semiconductive, leading to development of several electronic devices such as field effect transistors,[11] electric double-layer capacitors,[12] and sensors.[13,14] Despite various demonstrations of MOFs in chemical sensing, they have never been demonstrated in implantable biological sensing and integrated with flexible sensors to conduct multichannel determination of nutriments. In addition, the intrinsically brittle and rigid attribute of MOFs is mechanically incompatible with flexible electronics, leading to potential challenges in constructing MOF-based flexible devices. Thus, the unexplored but appealing properties associated with the combination of MOFs and flexible sensors for implantable applications demand in-depth investigation.
In this paper, we demonstrate materials and techniques to achieve flexible sensors integrated with MOFs. The flexible sensors are obtained through highly effective screen printing approaches, and are surface modified by thin films made of grinded copper-MOF (Cu-MOF) and cobalt-MOF (CoMOF) nanoparticles. The sensors can be used to detect trace of ascorbic acid (AA), l-Tryptophan (l-Trp), glycine, and glucose, all of which are nutriments that are closely involved in the metabolism and circulation processes. The sensors can operate under reduced bias voltage with prolonged working time for $20\mathrm{~d~}$ . Preliminary data using bioinspired tentacle-like multichannel sensors have also been presented, indicating the potential applications of such sensors in simultaneous determination of distribution and transportation of analytes in different organs and body locations. Experiments using living cells and animals demonstrate that the MOF-modified sensors are biologically safe and can detect l-Trp continuously in both blood and interstitial fluid. This work represents the first effort in integrating MOFs with flexible sensors to achieve highly specific and sensitive electrochemical detection, and resolves the clinic needs to understand nutriment distribution and transportation within different organs. The techniques and results presented may inspire appearance of more flexible electronic devices that utilize the optical, chemical, and electrical properties of MOFs for sensing, energy storage, and drug delivery applications. In addition, such bioinspired multichannel configuration when combining with other functional materials or different MOFs may yield new type of multifunctional devices with high spatial resolution to determine various chemical and physical parameters, while conducting optical or electrical stimulation to satisfy the clinical and scientific needs for monitoring nutriments and neuron transmitters in different organs and revealing the mechanisms of symptoms related to nutrient deficiency, amino acid metabolism disorders, and neurological disorders.
Figure 1a demonstrates the concept of the flexible sensors, which can determine nutriment distribution within different body organs or tissues to reveal transportation processes of nutriments after intake of food or injection of food substitutes using a single or multiple channel configuration. The flexible and ultrathin sensors allow simultaneous access to interstitial fluids and internal organs (such as brain, kidney, and bone) with complex geometry. The MOFs coated on electrodes can be used to improve sensitivity, stability, and longevity of the implantable devices. The capability to fine tune structures and compositions of MOFs allows to achieve different specificity for various nutriments. Figure 1b,c shows a schematic of a flexible electrochemical sensor integrated with MOFs. The entire sensor is situated on a flexible polyethylene terephthalate (PET) substrate coated with multiple-stacked layers made of silver, carbon, and polymeric insulator. These layers were printed sequentially using a high-throughput screen printer (Figure S1a, Supporting Information). The resulting sensor is $25\times3\times0.05~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ in dimension with a sharp tip to facilitate implantation into organs and tissues. MOFs can be immobilized on the working electrode ( $\mathrm{~\chi~}_{1}\ \mathrm{mm}$ in diameter) by drop casting using a well-dispersed solution made of MOFs and Nafion. The MOFs layer can cause redox reaction with the analytes due to existence of reactive metal site and offer large surface area of $778{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ to facilitate absorption of analytes and improve contact. In addition, the polymer ligands were made of thiophene derivative, which are electrically conductive to allow transportation of ions from the reaction site to the working electrode.
To characterize the electrochemical behavior, a Cu-MOFmodified flexible sensor was first tested using cyclic voltammetry (CV) in phosphate buffered saline (PBS) solution at $\mathrm{\pH=}$ 7.2. The results show significant oxidation and reduction peaks $(E_{\mathrm{pa}}=0.075~\mathrm{V}$ and $E_{\mathrm{pc}}=-0.174~\mathrm{V})$ due to reversible conversion from $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ . In contrast, the bare electrode only exhibits negligible peaks in buffer solution, demonstrating improved redox capability of the sensor after introduction of MOFs (Figure 1d). In addition, increased anodic $(\boldsymbol{i}_{\mathrm{pa}})$ and cathodic $({i_{\mathrm{pc}}})$ peak currents can be observed when a voltage scan rate changed from 0.02 to $0.14~\mathrm{V~s^{-1}}$ (Figure S1b, Supporting Information). The peak currents were later determined to be linearly proportional to the square roots of the scan rate (Figure S1c, Supporting Information), indicating that the electrochemical reaction was quasi-reversible and governed by free diffusion of the analytes in solutions.
MOFs are intrinsically brittle and rigid with robust mechanical properties such as the large hardness[15] ( $\b{H}=200$ MPa) and Young’s modulus[16] ( $\overset{\cdot}{E}=9.3$ GPa). As-synthesized MOFs contain large grains (Figure 1c), which is unable to achieve homogeneous solution when mixed with Nafion, and, thus, a flexible thin film that can tolerate repeated bending. Thus, the MOFs grains were milled into nanoparticles and dispersed to form a homogeneous solution (Figure 2a) that can be dropcasted to achieve highly flexible thin film (thickness $<10~\upmu\mathrm{m}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ) whose mechanical properties are compatible to the underlying layers. The ratio between MOFs and Nafion is also critical. As shown in Figure 2b–f, comparison study using five different ratios varied from $0\%$ to $25\%$ (percentage of Nafion in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ PBS) indicates that low concentrations of Nafion tend to cause film delamination, while high concentrations of Nafion lead to cracks on film. As a result, a ratio of $10\%$ was used throughout the experiment to offer stable thin films. As the MOFs solution was drop-casted onto the electrode manually, this may cause sensor variation, which demands individual calibration before conducting measurement. In addition, incomplete coverage of the MOFs layer on electrodes may hinder the transfer of the electrons, reduce the reaction area, and eventually influence the detection limit. However, the sensor variation has been largely minimized by using constant amount of casting solutions as well as an optimized mixing ratio between Nafion and MOFs to yield smoother coverage. The application of MOFs solution onto the sensors can be better controlled using approaches such as inkjet printing,[17] self-shaping,[18] and guiding templates.[19]
The flexible sensor can then be used to measure biological molecules (e.g., AA, amino acid, and other chemical compounds) that can be oxidized by reactive metal sites in MOFs (Figure 2g). A sensor modified by Cu-MOF was used to detect AA with various concentrations from 0 to $4\times10^{-3}$ m by CV measurement. The result shows more than six times increase in the magnitude of peak currents in Cu-MOF-modified sensor as compared with sensors with bare electrodes (Figure 2h), indicating that the presence of MOFs can enhance the redox reaction of AA. In addition, the bias voltage is only $0.06\mathrm{~V},$ which is much smaller than electrochemical detections using other known surface coating materials such as halloysite nanotubes[20] and gold nanoparticles,[21] suggesting that introducing MOFs in implantable flexible sensors may effectively avoid issues due to overvoltage that may cause water electrolysis and tissue damage and may reduce overall energy consumption. The $\mathbf{\xi}_{I-t}$ curve in Figure 2i obtained at the bias voltage of $0.06\mathrm{~V~}$ can be fitted, showing excellent linearity with a correlation coefficient (R) of 0.997 and a limit of determination of $14.97\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ .
The Cu-MOF-modified flexible sensor can also be used to measure l-Trp, which is an important amino acid involving in metabolism processes.[22] The CV response indicates the peaks of oxidation current appear at 0.794  V (Figure 2j). As shown in Figure $2\mathrm{k},$ the peak current of anode gradually increases with l-Trp concentrations with high linearity $(R~=~0.999)$ . The sensitivity of the Cu-MOF-modified sensor for l-Trp is
$1.12079\mathrm{mAM}^{-1}$ corresponding to a resolution of $4.14\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ . Additional detection using glycine that is widely employed for nutrition[23] and healthcare[24] demonstrates a resolution of $0.71\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ for glycine at a bias voltage of 0.21 V (Figure 2l) with excellent linearity (Figure S1d, Supporting Information). The different bias voltage in AA, l-Trp, and glycine measurement suggests an approach to distinguish and simultaneously measure these three chemicals under different bias voltages. The high sensitivity is favorable for implantable application due to trace amounts of these chemicals within organs.
As a comparison to the properties of Cu-MOF, a new kind of Co-MOF whose porous structure and synthesis (Table S1, Supporting Information) have never been reported is developed specifically for the flexible sensors (Figure 3a). Single crystal X-ray diffraction (XRD) reveals that Co-MOF crystallizes monoclinicaly with $\mathrm{P}2_{1}/n$ space group, in which each ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ is bridged by carboxyl into 1D chain that further forms 3D framework through hydrogen bonds (Figure 3b). The water stability of Co-MOF has also been studied using Powder XRD by immersing Co-MOF into water for over $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure 3c). The results show no noticeable degradation and dissolution of Co-MOF in water, and are compared similarly with as-synthesized Co-MOF and simulation results using Mercury, which is a crystal structure calibration software. As shown in Figure 3d, the thermogravimetric analysis of Co-MOF was also conducted, the weight loss of $20.16\%$ is close to the calculated value $(20.04\%)$ , and the framework could keep intact until about $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , showing a good thermal stability. When connecting to a lock-in-amplifier (SR830), the current–voltage $(I{-}V)$ curves (Figure S2, Supporting Information) of two pressed pellets made by Cu-MOF and Co-MOF can be obtained. The linear $I{-}V$ curves indicate the resistive effect of these two conductive MOFs. The conductivities of Cu-MOF and Co-MOF are then determined to be $3.05\times10^{-8}$ and $1.13\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , respectively. When mixing Cu-MOF and Co-MOF with glucose oxidase $(\mathrm{GO}_{x})$ to form glucose sensitive membrane, sensor modified with ${\bf C o-M O F/G O}_{x}$ shows better response than $\mathrm{Cu-MOF/GO}_{x}$ and pure ${\mathrm{GO}}_{x}$ (Figure 3e and Figure S3a, Supporting Information). This may be attributed to the better catalytic activity of cobalt-modified sensor than copper-modified one,[25] resulting in better capability to yield $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , while the very close surface area $(778\ m^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for
Cu-MOF and $704~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for Co-MOF) and pore size (2.5  Å for Cu-MOF and $1.57\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for Co-MOF) between Cu-MOF and Co-MOF make very small influence on this detection. The result also suggests that it is feasible to achieve better resolution and specificity by tuning the structure and composition of MOFs on the flexible sensors. The $\mathbf{\xi}_{I-t}$ curve of a CoMOF-modified electrode in different glucose concentrations in Figure S3b in the Supporting Information can be linearly fitted ( $R=0.997_{\cdot}$ ), resulting in a glucose detection resolution of $54.6\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ , which is sufficient to detect the hypoglycemia condition for diabetics. This Co-MOF-modified sensor offers a new alternative approach for glucose monitoring, and its resolution can be further improved by introducing MOFs with suitable pore sizes that allow trapping ${\mathrm{GO}}_{x}$ within the pores.
Various other tests were also conducted to demonstrate measurement capability of flexible sensors in different aspects. For example, to further demonstrate the capability of the sensor in adapting to different working conditions such as twisted blood vessels, nonuniform subcutaneous tissues, and complicated interior of organs, a sensor tied with a knot has been used to measure AA (Figure 3f). The sensor maintains high sensitivity and stability in AA measurement. The results compare similarly to the case with undeformed sensor (Figure 2h). As illustrated in Figure 3g, the selectivity of Cu-MOF-modified sensor was characterized by introducing AA at a bias voltage of $0.06{\mathrm{V}}.$ The sensor has nearly no response to glycine and histidine, and minimized response to l-Trp under this bias voltage. In addition, the sensor offers high repeatability when applying 0.1, 1, and $3\times10^{-3}$ m AA in turn into the microchamber (Figure 3h). The variation between each measurement with same concentrations is less than $14\%$ . An additional experiment using a fine step of $0.2\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}$ in Figure S4 in the Supporting Information also demonstrates excellent repeatability in lower analyte concentrations. Due to the elimination of biological active components, the longevity of MOF-modified flexible sensor can be improved. The sensor can work continuously over a period at least 20  d (Figure 3i). The overall change of magnitude over the time period is within $30~\mathrm{{nA}}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Extended working period is also feasible by tuning the structure of MOFs, such as the ratio of metal sites and organic ligands, or replacing metal ions that have better redox capability to obtain better reversibility.
The cytotoxicity study of sensor integrated with MOFs was conducted using Calcein-AM/PI double-stain assay. The corresponding fluorescent images in Figure $4\mathrm{a-c}$ shows the viable (green) and dead (red) cells dyed by Calcein-AM/PI double-stain assay after Cu-MOF, Co-MOF, and bare electrodes were immersed for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The images show that there are more than $99\%$ of living cells with and without the presence of MOFs, suggesting that the MOF-modified sensors are biologically safe. The cytotoxicity was also conducted using 3-(4,5-dimethyl-2-thiazolyl)- 2,5-diphenyl-2-H-tetrazolium bromide (MTT) assay, the cells viability within the culture solution immersed with sensors modified with Cu-MOF and Co-MOF is $95\%$ and $84\%$ compared with the control group (Figure 4d), satisfying the required $70\%$ cell viability as recommended by USP(ISO 10993-5).[26] The better cell viability of Cu-MOF may suggest that stable Cu-MOF may be more biocompatible to cells. Overexposure to Co-MOF may cause negative health effect, which demands further long-term study.
An animal experiment was further conducted to evaluate the sensor performance in vivo. Two Cu-MOF-modified sensors were used to measure interstitial fluid and venous blood, respectively. One of the sensors obtained venous blood from caudal vein by cutting the tail of the mouse (Figure 4e), while the other was implanted into the abdominal cavity of the mouse to access interstitial fluid directly (Figure $^{4\mathrm{g})}$ . The l-Trp concentrations within the interstitial fluid and the blood were manually alternated by giving a high dose of $\mathrm{~L~}$ -Trp through intraperitoneal injection. The measurement results obtained from the venous blood (Figure 4f) and the interstitial fluid (Figure 4h) both exhibit significant changes in CV curve before and after injection, with a peak anodic current at $0.79{\mathrm{V}}.$ As illustrated in Figure 4i, the peak anodic current $(\boldsymbol{i}_{\mathrm{pa}})$ increased gradually after injection of l-Trp, and then a marked oxidation peak appeared after injection for $6\mathrm{min}$ . The concentration of l-Trp maintained relatively stable over a measurement period about $5~\mathrm{min}$ . These results indicate that the MOF-modified flexible sensor is suitable for implantable applications for detecting various chemicals in blood and interstitial fluids.
This paper presents materials and techniques to integrate MOFs with flexible sensors to achieve highly sensitive and stable biological sensing. The MOF-modified sensor can be potentially used for implantable continuous monitoring of nutriments within organs, tissues, and interstitial fluids. The rigid MOFs particles can be milled into nanoparticles and uniformly dispersed into solution, allowing formation of thin films that is mechanical compatible to the flexible sensors. Conductive Cu-MOF and Co-MOF have been developed with various measurement capabilities to different analytes. The results have demonstrated excellent measurement resolutions in determining different biological samples with good specificity, reversibility, and longevity. The toxicity of MOFs and in vivo measurement have also been demonstrated, showing great promise toward stable and reliable long-term sensing to satisfy clinic and research needs to quantify nutrient distribution and transportation.
Further investigation using optimized composition and structures of MOFs may yield devices with higher performance, leading to improved conductivity and measurement sensitivity as well as selectivity. Besides of using MOFs to facilitate electrochemical detection for flexible sensors, more devices based on different sensing and working mechanism can be developed using different optical, electrical, and chemical properties of MOFs. This work suggests a potential new strategy to combine MOFs with flexible electronic technology, leading to promising solutions to tackle various issues in energy, healthcare, and other sensing or monitoring applications.
## Experimental Section
Sensor Fabrication and Preparation: The fabrication of the sensors started with printing Ag conductive ink (Shenzhen sunflower electronic material Co., Ltd.) onto a PET film ( $50~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in thickness, CHENG LIDA Co., Ltd.) using a screen-printing machine (PHP-2020A) to form three electrodes and conductive connections $(5~\upmu\mathsf{m}$ in thickness). The carbon working and counter electrodes were then printed on to existing $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}$ electrodes, following by screen printing an insulation layer (DENING SIYIN Co., Ltd.). All printed layers were subject to a curing process at ${\mathsf{120}}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ (for $\mathsf{A g}$ and insulation layers) or $\mathsf{l}50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (for carbon layer) for $\rceil\mathfrak{h}$ The reference electrode made of $\mathsf{A g}$ was then chloridized by dipping the printed $\mathsf{A g}$ areas into $\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}$ $(50\times70^{-3}~\mathrm{m})$ for $50\ s,\AA^{[27]}$ allowing formation of $\mathsf{A g C l}$ on $\mathsf{A g}$ layer. To enhance the conductivity of carbon layer, carbon conductive ink (Jining Leadernano Tech.) was doped with graphene $(7~\mathrm{wt\%})$ before screen printing.
MOFs dispersion solution was obtained by mixing grinded MOFs micro-/nanoparticles $_{(0.02\mathrm{~g)~}}$ with PBS ( $70m L$ , Biological Industries) premixed with $10\mathrm{\ut\%}$ of Nafion $(5~\mathrm{wt}\%$ in lower aliphatic alcohols and water, Sigma-Aldrich). The solution was then sonicated for $10\min$ using a high power sonicator (SCIENTZ-750F from Ningbo Scientz Biotechnology Co., Ltd.), resulting in a highly viscous and homogenous solution, which was then drop-casted onto the working electrode of the flexible sensor using a micropipette. Additional layer of Nafion $(7~\upmu\up L)$ was also drop-casted onto the existing MOFs layer to cover MOFs particles. To prepare the sensor for glucose detection, mixture of glucose oxidase solution $(80\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ in PBS of $\mathsf{P H}7.2$ , Meilun Biotech) and carbon nanotube solution in the ratio 10:1 (volume by volume) were drop-casted onto the MOFs layer and dried overnight at $4^{\circ}C$ prior to the application of Nafion layer.
Synthesis of $C u$ -MOF and Co-MOF: Synthesis of $\mathsf{C u}$ -MOF was conducted using a solvothermal method as mentioned in a previous paper,[13] resulting in green crystals with average pore size of $2.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ Powder XRD pattern of $\mathsf{C u}$ -MOF was measured using a $\mathsf{D}/\mathsf{M a x}{-2500}$ X-ray diffractometer with Cu $\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation (Figure S6, Supporting Information). The Co-MOF was achieved using following procedures. A mixture of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O A c}})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ $(0.2\times70^{-3}~\mathrm{m}$ , $49.8\ \mathrm{mg})$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{L}$ $(0.2\times\mathsf{l}0^{-3}\ \mathsf{m},$ $46.04~\mathrm{mg};$ ), and $20~{\upmu\mathsf{L}}~{\mathsf{H N O}}_{3}$ in a mixture of $H_{2}O$ $(6~\mathsf{m L})$ and N,Ndimethylacetamide (DMA) $(6~\mathsf{m L})$ were sealed in a $75m L$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave. The autoclave was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 1  d under autogenous pressure and then cooled to room temperature $5^{\circ}C\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ under ambient conditions. Red Co-MOF crystals with average pore size of $\lvert1.57\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ were eventually obtained after thoroughly washed by DMA. The yield of Co-MOF was estimated to be $85\%$ basing on $C_{^{\mathsf{O}}}({\mathsf{O A c}})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ , and the corresponding elemental analysis (EA) using XRD indicated that the contents of C and H in synthesized samples of Co-MOF were $26.66\%$ and $3.46\%$ , respectively. The EA accorded well with the result by calculation, in which C and H were determined to be $26.75\%$ and $3.37\%$ , respectively. The calculation process can be briefly described as below: $C\%=(72\times N C)/\mathsf{M}=26.75\%$ , $H\%=(7\times N H)/$ $M\ =\ 3.37\%$ , where NC represents the number of C atom in per molecule of Co-MOF; NH represents the number of H atom in per molecule of Co-MOF; M represents the weight of per molecule of Co-MOF.
Electrochemical Measurements: All electrochemical measurements were conducted using an electrochemical workstation (CHI660E). Before immobilization of MOFs, CV tests were carried out at printed electrodes in $k_{3}[\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]$ solution for at least 20 circles until a stable CV curve was obtained. The electrochemical properties of the MOFs sensors were characterized using CV measurement at different scan rates from 0.02 to $0.14\lor\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ with a potential range set from $-0.4$ to $0.4~\mathsf{V}.$ For the measurement of the selectivity of AA, the amperometric $\scriptstyle{I-t}$ curve was recorded when AA $(0.5\times70^{-3}\mathrm{~M})$ , glycine $(0.5\times70^{-3}\mathrm{~M})$ , histidine $(0.5\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M})$ , and l-Trp $(0.5\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M})$ were added to the phosphate buffer saline in order at a bias voltage of $0.06~\mathsf{V}.$ The reversibility of the sensors was obtained at two concentration ranges by measuring $\mathbf{\xi}_{I-t}$ curve at a bias voltage of 0.1  V. The sensor was firstly used to measure AA (0.1, 1, and $\bar{3}\times\mathsf{l}0^{-3}\mathrm{~m~}$ , repeatedly). Then AA with different concentrations (0.1, 0.3, and $0.5\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}$ ) were sensed repeatedly to characterize the reversibility at low concentration. The I–t curve was recorded during the test. The longevity was obtained by measuring chronoamperometric response of AA continuously for $20~\mathrm{d}$ .
Cytotoxicity Tests for MOFs Biosensor: To obtain the cytotoxicity of MOFs biosensor, sensors coated with $\mathsf{C u}$ -MOF and Co-MOF, respectively, were immersed into cell culture solution after sanitized with alcohol and short UV light exposure. The cell culture solution was filled with $293\ensuremath{\mathsf{T}}$ cells. The cells were dyed using Calcein-AM/PI double-stain kit (Calcein-AM from Aladdin, propidium iodide (PI) from Solarbio) after $24\ h$ sensor immersion. The cytotoxicity was determined by the fluorescent images of the dyed viable and dead cells obtained by Olympus IX51. The cell viability was also evaluated by the MTT assay. MTT assay $(70\upmu\updownarrow,0.5\%)$ (Solarbio) was added to the cell culture solution after $24\ h$ sensor immersion. Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) $(\mathsf{l}00~\mathsf{\upmu L})$ was added to the culture solution $4h$ later and then oscillated for 10 min to dissolve the crystals completely. The cell viability was determined by a Multiskan FC Microplate Reader at $560~\mathsf{n m}$ absorbance.
In Vivo l-Trp Measurement Experiment: All were conducted in Tianjin Institute of Environmental $\&$ Operational Medicine with accreditation number SYXK (M) 2014-0004 under the ethic and operation guideline required by the institute. The institutional committees of Tianjin Institute of Environmental $\&$ Operational Medicine have approved the animal experiments. In vivo measurement was conducted using a $B A L B/c$ mouse $(\mathbb{19.2}\ \mathrm{g})$ . The mouse was anesthetized by injecting chloral hydrate ( $190\upmu\mathrm{L},4\up w t\%)$ . A dose of l-Trp solution $(7.5\times70^{-3}\ \mathrm{{M}},$ $200~\upmu\upiota$ in volume) was given during the test by intraperitoneal injection to alter the l-Trp concentration in the blood and the interstitial fluid. Two sensors were used to measure the l-Trp in blood and interstitial fluid, respectively. The venous blood was obtained from the tail vein and drop-casted onto the sensor modified with $\mathsf{C u}$ -MOF for electrochemical measurement before and after the injection of l-Trp. At the same time, the other sensor was inserted into the abdominal cavity of the mouse to continuously monitor l-Trp in interstitial fluid of the mouse. The scan voltage was set from $-0.6$ to $\textsf{l V}$ at a scan rate of $80~\mathrm{mV}~\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ .
